<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100" phx-no-curly-interpolation>
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="connect-docker-sandboxes"
      updated="September 9, 2026"
      evidence="The MCP gateway and an audited action were live-tested with Docker Sandboxes 0.39.0 on macOS on September 7, 2026; a signed-in agent was not part of that test."
      toc={[
        {"start", "Start the connection"},
        {"install", "Install the host tools"},
        {"launcher", "Create the host launcher"},
        {"register", "Register emisar"},
        {"run", "Run the agent"},
        {"verify", "Verify the connection"},
        {"limits-and-risks", "Limits & risks"},
        {"cleanup", "Remove the setup"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="connect-docker-sandboxes">
        <:dek>
          Run a coding agent in a Docker microVM and give it emisar through Docker's MCP gateway.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Docker Sandboxes isolates the agent from most of your host. The emisar bridge stays on the
        host, where Docker's gateway can reach it without copying your emisar key into the VM.
        If you want the shortest local setup, start with <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/connect-coop"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >co:op</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          Docker Sandboxes installed on a supported macOS or Ubuntu host, and a Docker account.
          Follow Docker's <.link
            href="https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/install/"
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >installation guide</.link>.
          This walkthrough uses a POSIX host launcher; Windows needs an equivalent owner-only
          launcher.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          A coding agent supported by <.docs_inline_code>sbx run</.docs_inline_code>, signed in on
          the host, and a project directory containing only files the agent should be able to edit.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          An emisar account with permission to connect an agent and at least one runner online.
          The
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >quickstart</.link>
          sets up a runner and its first action.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_h2 id="start">1. Start the connection</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open <a
          href={~p"/app/agents/connect"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Connect an agent</a>, choose <strong class="text-zinc-200">Custom</strong>, and create a
        dedicated key named <strong class="text-zinc-200">Docker Sandboxes</strong>. Keep the page
        open: the key is shown only once.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="install">2. Install the host tools</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Install Docker Sandboxes with Docker's platform instructions, then sign in and install the
        emisar bridge on the host. The
        <.docs_inline_code>--yes</.docs_inline_code>
        flag installs the bridge without trying to configure a host agent.
      </p>
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="host shell"
        copy_text="sbx login\ncurl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh | sudo bash -s -- --yes\nemisar-mcp --version"
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sbx login
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh | sudo bash -s -- --yes
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> emisar-mcp --version</.docs_code>

      <.docs_h2 id="launcher">3. Create the host launcher</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Keep the key and launcher outside the project directory shared with the VM. Create
        <.docs_inline_code>~/.config/emisar/docker-sandboxes/bridge.env</.docs_inline_code>
        with the values below, replacing the key with the one from step 1.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="bridge.env">EMISAR_URL=https://emisar.dev
EMISAR_API_KEY=emk-replace-with-your-key
EMISAR_CLIENT=docker-sandboxes</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Save this executable as <.docs_inline_code>~/.config/emisar/docker-sandboxes/launch-emisar</.docs_inline_code>:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="launch-emisar">#!/bin/sh
set -a
. "$HOME/.config/emisar/docker-sandboxes/bridge.env"
set +a
exec /usr/local/bin/emisar-mcp</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Restrict both files to your user. Docker will run the launcher on the host, outside the
        sandbox.
      </p>
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="host shell"
        copy_text="chmod 700 ~/.config/emisar/docker-sandboxes\nchmod 600 ~/.config/emisar/docker-sandboxes/bridge.env\nchmod 700 ~/.config/emisar/docker-sandboxes/launch-emisar"
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> chmod 700 ~/.config/emisar/docker-sandboxes
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> chmod 600 ~/.config/emisar/docker-sandboxes/bridge.env
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> chmod 700 ~/.config/emisar/docker-sandboxes/launch-emisar</.docs_code>

      <.docs_h2 id="register">4. Register emisar</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Register the trusted launcher as a local MCP server, then check the registration. The list
        should show
        <.docs_inline_code>emisar</.docs_inline_code>
        as a local command.
      </p>
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="host shell"
        copy_text="sbx mcp add emisar --command $HOME/.config/emisar/docker-sandboxes/launch-emisar\nsbx mcp ls"
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sbx mcp add emisar --command "$HOME/.config/emisar/docker-sandboxes/launch-emisar"
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sbx mcp ls</.docs_code>

      <.docs_h2 id="run">5. Run the agent</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        From the project directory, start a new sandbox with emisar preloaded in static MCP mode.
        This example uses Codex; replace
        <.docs_inline_code>codex</.docs_inline_code>
        with another agent listed in Docker's <.link
          href="https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/sbx/create/"
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >supported agent commands</.link>.
      </p>
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="host shell"
        copy_text="sbx run codex --name emisar-agent --static-mcp emisar ."
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sbx run codex --name emisar-agent --static-mcp emisar .</.docs_code>

      <.docs_h2 id="verify">6. Verify the connection</.docs_h2>
      <ol class="mt-5 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>Confirm that the agent lists the emisar MCP tools.</li>
        <li>
          Ask: <strong class="font-medium text-zinc-200">Use emisar to find a runner with
          linux.uptime, run it, and show me the output.</strong>
        </li>
        <li>
          Complete any approval required by your <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >emisar policy</.link>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Open
          <a
            href={~p"/app/audit"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Audit</a>
          and confirm the action, runner, operator, and result.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h2 id="limits-and-risks">Limits &amp; risks</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-5 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          Docker shares the project directory with the VM. Remove secret files and mount only the
          directories the agent needs.
        </li>
        <li>
          The registered MCP command runs on the host with your permissions. Keep the launcher and
          environment file out of the shared project and never let the agent edit them.
        </li>
        <li>
          The emisar key and its rotated successor remain on the host. The VM reaches the bridge
          through Docker's gateway; it does not need the raw key.
        </li>
        <li>
          The VM can have its own Docker socket. That is not the host Docker socket, but code in the
          VM can control containers inside the VM.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="cleanup">Remove the setup</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        When you no longer need this sandbox, remove its VM and MCP registration. Then revoke the
        dedicated key from
        <a
          href={~p"/app/agents"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >AI agents</a>
        before deleting the owner-only launcher files.
      </p>
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="host shell"
        copy_text="sbx rm emisar-agent\nsbx mcp rm emisar"
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sbx rm emisar-agent
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sbx mcp rm emisar</.docs_code>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
